1323 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 292 0 R
/E (The framing matters. Security here is not a backdrop to the fiscal argument; it is another facet of the same absorptive-capacity variable. A state that cannot hold territory cannot tax it, cannot govern the mining economy within it, and cannot convert the assets inside it into anything but a prize for whoever is armed. This is why insecurity qualifies the Jigawa comparison \(Chapter 12\) and why it cannot simply be added to the fiscal analysis as a separate problem: it is the security-provision facet of the capacity bundle defined in Chapter 04, and it fails for the same governance reasons the fiscal facets do.)
/Pg 53 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1324 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 292 0 R
/E (The report uses Amotekun as a Nigerian near-peer for funding and governance lessons, while South African and Indian cases illustrate specialist mining-security functions.)
/Pg 53 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
